on the 13th day of the dark half of the month of Ashwin, a flag-changing ceremony is held. It is said that a woman of this village took diksha and became Sadhvi Sangyashriji. When she was undertaking a long fast in Ahmedabad, her final wish was to visit this temple. She breathed her last while praying to Shri Saptaphana Parshwanath Bhagwan of her home village. That very evening, the temple priest saw this image being worshipped in Bhansal -- an event truly regarded as a miracle.. Directions: Bhansal is 24 kilometers from Jamnagar. There is only one Jain household here. There is an upashraya and a panjrapole (animal shelter) here. Jamnagar has many very famous temples. The Navanagar Neminath Bhagwan pilgrimage site is 3 km from Jamnagar. This image of Bhagwan Neminath was worshipped by Lord Neminath's cousins, Krishna and Baldev. There is lodging available, but no bhojanshala. Pilgrims who visit here are given provisions (bhata).. Scripture References: Shri Saptaphana Parshwanath is mentioned in \\\"365 Shri Parshwa Jin Nammala,\\\" the Parshwanath Nammala, \\\"108 Naam Garbhit Shri Parshwanath Stavan,\\\" and \\\"Shri Parshwanath Sankhya Stavan.\\\" The poet has called Saptaphana Parshwanath the \\\"Kamit Phal Datar\\\" (granter of desired fruits).. Hymn of Praise: O Lord Saptaphana Parshwa, dispel my seven fears; O Lord, teach my mind the path in Bhalsan; devotees who wish for your darshan come here as if arriving as gods themselves. \\\"I bow with devotion to Shri Saptaphana Parshwa.\\\". Chanting Mantra: Om Hrim Arham Shri Saptaphana Parshwanathay Namah.. Trust: Shri Saptaphana Parshwanath Shwetambar Jain Tirth, Panjrapole, Post: Moti Bhansal, District: Jamnagar (Saurashtra), Gujarat-361012, India. Phone: 0288-2610244, Mobile: 09099214724, 09924348845 (Mahendra Bhai, Manager), 09409411306 (Raju Bhai Vora, Trustee).. Some further verses of the hymn to Shri Shankheshwara Parshwanath are also recorded here, praising the Lord's depth, radiance and grace, offering salutations to Shankheshwar Parshwanath.. 63. Shri Bhabha Parshwanath. Main Deity: A black-colored image of Bhagwan Shri Bhabha Parshwanath, 47 inches tall and 40 inches wide, in the padmasana posture. Above the head of the image is a canopy of 7 serpent hoods. This image is made of sand.. Location: This is in Jamnagar, Saurashtra, at Chokdi, known as the Chorivala Derasar. There are 2 other temples of Bhabha Parshwanath -- one in Patan and another in Ahmedabad.. History: Jamnagar is an ancient and important city of Saurashtra, and also a cantonment town. Shri Vardhaman Shah, Shri Padamsingh Seth, Shri Tejasvi Seth and Shri Raisingh Seth came from Bhadreshwar and settled in Jamnagar. These four Shreshthis built a beautiful and grand temple here, known as the Chorivala Temple. The attractive image of Shri Parshwanath was consecrated here (Anjanshalaka and Pratishtha) by the revered hands of Achalgacchiya Acharya Shri Kalyansagarsurishwarji, on Sunday, Vaishakh Sud Ashtami, Vikram Samvat 1678. In North Gujarat and Saurashtra, the eldest member of a household is honored with the respectful title \\\"Bha.\\\" Adding this highly respectful term to the name of Bhagwan Parshwanath as \\\"Bhabha,\\\" in order to express the greatest possible honor and respect, this image came to be addressed as \\\"Shri Bhabha Parshwanath,\\\" and hence this pilgrimage site is called Shri Bhabha Parshwanath. Most of the Jain community of Jamnagar live abroad, but they have used their wealth for all religious activities. They have built many temples in all the villages of Jamnagar.. Other Temples: There are 16 temples here.. Art and Sculpture: The temples here are very grand, artistic, striking and beautiful. The carving in the temple is highly artistic and recalls the temples of Shatrunjay Mahatirth. The images in the temple are enchanting and captivating. The colored artwork in the temple is very striking.. Directions: Jamnagar is connected by rail and road to all cities of Gujarat. Buses and private vehicles are available here. The Navanagar pilgrimage site, where the main deity is Shri Neminath Bhagwan, is 3 km from here. Dharamshala and bhojanshala facilities are available here. There is a very good and well-known Sanskrit school here, where many monks and nuns come to learn Sanskrit. The Jain community here is very devout, and many Chhari Palit Sanghs (foot pilgrimages) come here, and elaborate austerities (tapasya) are regularly organized.. Scripture References: Bhabha Parshwanath is mentioned in \\\"365 Shri Parshwa Jin Nammala,\\\" \\\"Shri Shankheshwar Parshwanath Chhand,\\\" \\\"Shri Bhagwan Parshwanath Stavan,\\\" \\\"Shri Parshwanath Nammala,\\\" \\\"Shri Parshwanath Sankhya Stavan,\\\" \\\"108 Parshwanath ke Chhand,\\\" and elsewhere. There is an image of Bhabha Parshwanath at Kareda Tirth and at the Kalikund Parshwanath temple in Santacruz, Mumbai.. 1. The main temple of Bhabha Parshwanath is in Jamnagar, Saurashtra, Gujarat. 2. In Patan, there is an ancient temple of Bhabha Parshwanath at Bhabha no Pado, where Bhabha Parshwanath is the Mulanayak. The attractive image is believed to date from the time of King Sampratti. The temple's dome is filled with skillful architecture. The previous ten births of Bhagwan Parshwanath are beautifully depicted, with cloth paintings (pat) of various pilgrimage sites, done in gold tones, that catch the eye. Because of radium paint, the pat glow fluorescent at night. Elders say that at midnight the sound of bells and music used to be heard, and this divine temple was filled with the fragrance of incense and flowers. This temple was last renovated in V.S. 2015. The upashraya has a vast knowledge repository with rare and ancient books. 3. In Ahmedabad there is an ancient temple of Bhabha Parshwanath, in which the image of the Lord is small and pleasing to the eye.. Hymn of Praise: Your well-formed body dispels the afflictions of karma; embracing you in totality, O Parshwa Bhabha, you shine forth; awakening the souls of Jamnagar, spreading friendship (maitri) -- \\\"I bow with devotion to Shri Bhabha Parshwa.\\\". Chanting Mantra: Om Hrim Arham Shri Bhabha Parshwanathay Namah.. Trust: Shri Bhabha Parshwanath Shwetambar Jain Tirth, Seth Rishi Vardhaman Jain Pedhi, Chorivala Derasar, Post: Jamnagar, District: Saurashtra, State: Gujarat-360001, India. Phone: 0288-267840/2678923/2555946.. History of the 108 Parshwanathdada and How to Reach Them, Part 15: Having completed pilgrimages to a total of 63 shrines of Lord Parshwanath -- Navsari, Surat, Rander, Bharuch, Gandhar, Dabhoi, Chhani, Borsad, Khambhat, Kheda, Dholka, Ahmedabad, Naroda, Titoi, Modar, Baroda, Nana Posina, Mota Posina, Vijapur, Vihar, Visnagar, Mehsana, Sidhpur, Dhota Saklana, Palanpur, Bhildiyaji, Umbri, Charup, Patan (9), Mansa, Mumbai, Gambhu, Chanasma, Kambhoi, Munjpur, Shankheshwar, Shankhalpur, Mandal, Sherisa, Ghogha, Ajahara, Prabhas Patan, Chorwad, Mangrol, Bareja, Jambhanvad, and Moti Bhansal and Jamnagar -- we will now go on to visit 2 more shrines of Lord Parshwanath, one each at Bhadreshwar and Suthri. With these, the pilgrimage of 63 shrines in Gujarat and 2 in Rajasthan, totaling 65 shrines of Lord Parshwanath, is complete. From here we shall travel onward toward Rajasthan. Gandhidham to Bhadreshwar is 33 km, Bhadreshwar to Suthri is 127 km. There is regular rail service to Gandhidham from Ahmedabad.. Bhadreshwar: 64. Shri Bhadreshwar Parshwanath. Suthri: 65. Shri Dhritkallol Parshwanath.. Purushadani Arhat Parshwanath Charitra, Part 9 (Shri Parshwakumar's Tour of the City): One day the prince was sitting in the palace courtyard, observing the city. He saw a great crowd of people carrying worship offerings and heading out of the city. The curious Parshwakumar asked, \\\"Is there some festival today, or some other special occasion? Where are these people going?\\\" He was told, \\\"O Prince! Outside the city lives a great ascetic named Kamath, who is performing the panchagni tapas (five-fire austerity). He is a very fierce ascetic, and these people are going to worship him.\\\". The curious prince Parshwa also went to see Kamath. This Kamath was the very same figure whose connection with Parshwanath's life had continued across many births. As soon as the prince, through his avadhignan (clairvoyant knowledge), saw a snake burning in the fire-pit along with the wood, he ordered his servant to remove the burning log from the fire and carefully split it open. When the burning log was split open, a pair of snakes -- male and female -- was found writhing, half-burnt, in their final moments of life. Parshwakumar had his servant recite the Navkar Mahamantra to them. They died in a state of meditative peace (samadhi) and became Dharanendra (the Indra of the Nagakumar class of gods). People began to despise and scorn Kamath.\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"1b:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mb-8 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"a\\\",null,{\\\"href\\\":\\\"https://goo.gl/maps/wVxtCTjHQo5S6ACE7\\\",\\\"target\\\":\\\"_blank\\\",\\\"rel\\\":\\\"noopener noreferrer\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-sm bg-maroon px-5 py-2.5 font-ui text-sm font-medium text-ivory hover:bg-maroon-deep transition-colors\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L1a\\\",null,{\\\"ref\\\":\\\"$undefined\\\",\\\"iconNode\\\":\\\"$8:2:props:children:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:iconNode\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"lucide-map-pin h-4 w-4\\\",\\\"aria-hidden\\\":true}],\\\"Open in Google Maps\\\"]}],null,[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L20\\\",null,{\\\"text\\\":\\\"$21\\\",\\\"lang\\\":\\\"en\\\",\\\"label\\\":\\\"Listen\\\",\\\"stopLabel\\\":\\\"Stop\\\"}]]}]\\n\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"1c:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mb-10 grid grid-cols-2 gap-2 sm:grid-cols-3\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",\\\"164a616e-694e-4fe8-807e-b059a7bcc451\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"relative overflow-hidden rounded-sm bg-sandstone-deep/30 col-span-2 aspect-[4/3] sm:col-span-2\\\",\\\"children\\\":[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L22\\\",null,{\\\"src\\\":\\\"https://iiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/tirtha-images/img_11934.jpeg\\\",\\\"alt\\\":\\\"Shri Saptaphana Parshwanath and Shri Bhabha Parshwanath फोटो 1\\\",\\\"fill\\\":true,\\\"sizes\\\":\\\"(min-width: 640px) 33vw, 50vw\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"object-cover\\\"}]}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",\\\"a14687a0-97ad-42bc-9548-5a7b3f64252f\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"relative overflow-hidden rounded-sm bg-sandstone-deep/30 aspect-square\\\",\\\"children\\\":[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L22\\\",null,{\\\"src\\\":\\\"https://iiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/tirtha-images/img_11979.jpeg\\\",\\\"alt\\\":\\\"Shri Saptaphana Parshwanath and Shri Bhabha Parshwanath फोटो 2\\\",\\\"fill\\\":true,\\\"sizes\\\":\\\"(min-width: 640px) 33vw, 50vw\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"object-cover\\\"}]}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",\\\"4fd569d1-0b77-4f7d-9670-262a1336b19d\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"relative overflow-hidden rounded-sm bg-sandstone-deep/30 aspect-square\\\",\\\"children\\\":[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L22\\\",null,{\\\"src\\\":\\\"https://iiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/tirtha-images/img_11985.jpeg\\\",\\\"alt\\\":\\\"Shri Saptaphana Parshwanath and Shri Bhabha Parshwanath फोटो 3\\\",\\\"fill\\\":true,\\\"sizes\\\":\\\"(min-width: 640px) 33vw, 50vw\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"object-cover\\\"}]}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",\\\"b7788009-9a51-401b-985a-cde4edc57da9\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"relative overflow-hidden rounded-sm bg-sandstone-deep/30 aspect-square\\\",\\\"children\\\":[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L22\\\",null,{\\\"src\\\":\\\"https://iiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/tirtha-images/img_11940.jpeg\\\",\\\"alt\\\":\\\"Shri Saptaphana Parshwanath and Shri Bhabha Parshwanath फोटो 4\\\",\\\"fill\\\":true,\\\"sizes\\\":\\\"(min-width: 640px) 33vw, 50vw\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"object-cover\\\"}]}]]}]\\n\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"23:T432,History: During the 16th century, many Jains from the Parkar region came and settled in Bhansal. They were very prosperous and wealthy. Among them were two Shreshthis, Raisi Shah and Vardhaman Shah, whose businesses were worth crores. These two Shreshthis built a beautiful and attractive temple, and in Vikram Samvat 1540 the images of several Tirthankaras were installed in it. One such image was of Bhagwan Parshwanath, which became the temple's Mulanayak image, with an attractive canopy of 7 serpent hoods above its head. This image is known as Shri Saptaphana Parshwanath. Every year, on the 13th day of the dark half of the month of Ashwin, a flag-changing ceremony is held. It is said that a woman of this village took diksha and became Sadhvi Sangyashriji. When she was undertaking a long fast in Ahmedabad, her final wish was to visit this temple. She breathed her last while praying to Shri Saptaphana Parshwanath Bhagwan of her home village. That very evening, the temple priest saw this image being worshipped in Bhansal -- an event truly regarded as a miracle.\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"1d:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"article\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"prose-none space-y-4 font-body text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.85] text-ink\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"0\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"62. Shri Saptaphana Parshwanath\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"1\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"This temple is located in Bhansala (Moti Bhansal), District Jamnagar (Saurashtra).\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"2\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Main Deity: A white-colored image of Shri Saptaphana Parshwanath, 20 inches tall and 16.25 inches wide, in the padmasana posture, with a canopy of 7 serpent hoods above its head.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"3\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Location: This is in Bhansal village, in Jamnagar district, Saurashtra.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"4\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"$23\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"5\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Directions: Bhansal is 24 kilometers from Jamnagar. There is only one Jain household here. There is an upashraya and a panjrapole (animal shelter) here. Jamnagar has many very famous temples. The Navanagar Neminath Bhagwan pilgrimage site is 3 km from Jamnagar. This image of Bhagwan Neminath was worshipped by Lord Neminath's cousins, Krishna and Baldev. There is lodging available, but no bhojanshala. Pilgrims who visit here are given provisions (bhata).\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"6\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Scripture References: Shri Saptaphana Parshwanath is mentioned in \\\\\\\"365 Shri Parshwa Jin Nammala,\\\\\\\" the Parshwanath Nammala, \\\\\\\"108 Naam Garbhit Shri Parshwanath Stavan,\\\\\\\" and \\\\\\\"Shri Parshwanath Sankhya Stavan.\\\\\\\" The poet has called Saptaphana Parshwanath the \\\\\\\"Kamit Phal Datar\\\\\\\" (granter of desired fruits).\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"7\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Hymn of Praise: O Lord Saptaphana Parshwa, dispel my seven fears; O Lord, teach my mind the path in Bhalsan; devotees who wish for your darshan come here as if arriving as gods themselves. \\\\\\\"I bow with devotion to Shri Saptaphana Parshwa.\\\\\\\"\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"8\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Chanting Mantra: Om Hrim Arham Shri Saptaphana Parshwanathay Namah.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"9\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Trust: Shri Saptaphana Parshwanath Shwetambar Jain Tirth, Panjrapole, Post: Moti Bhansal, District: Jamnagar (Saurashtra), Gujarat-361012, India. Phone: 0288-2610244, Mobile: 09099214724, 09924348845 (Mahendra Bhai, Manager), 09409411306 (Raju Bhai Vora, Trustee).\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"10\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Some further verses of the hymn to Shri Shankheshwara Parshwanath are also recorded here, praising the Lord's depth, radiance and grace, offering salutations to Shankheshwar Parshwanath.\\\"}],\\\"$L24\\\",\\\"$L25\\\",\\\"$L26\\\",\\\"$L27\\\",\\\"$L28\\\",\\\"$L29\\\",\\\"$L2a\\\",\\\"$L2b\\\",\\\"$L2c\\\",\\\"$L2d\\\",\\\"$L2e\\\",\\\"$L2f\\\",\\\"$L30\\\",\\\"$L31\\\",\\\"$L32\\\",\\\"$L33\\\"]}]\\n\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"1e:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"nav\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mt-14 flex items-center justify-between border-t border-sandstone-deep/60 pt-6\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L5\\\",null,{\\\"href\\\":\\\"/en/tirtha/tirtha-080\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft hover:text-maroon transition-colors\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L1a\\\",null,{\\\"ref\\\":\\\"$undefined\\\",\\\"iconNode\\\":[[\\\"path\\\",{\\\"d\\\":\\\"m15 18-6-6 6-6\\\",\\\"key\\\":\\\"1wnfg3\\\"}]],\\\"className\\\":\\\"lucide-chevron-left h-4 w-4\\\",\\\"aria-hidden\\\":true}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"max-w-[10rem] truncate sm:max-w-xs\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"Shri Amritzhara Parshwanath\\\"}]]}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L5\\\",null,{\\\"href\\\":\\\"/en/tirtha/tirtha-082\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 text-right font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft hover:text-maroon transition-colors\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"max-w-[10rem] truncate sm:max-w-xs\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"Shri Bhadreshwar (Shyamala) Parshwanath\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L1a\\\",null,{\\\"ref\\\":\\\"$undefined\\\",\\\"iconNode\\\":[[\\\"path\\\",{\\\"d\\\":\\\"m9 18 6-6-6-6\\\",\\\"key\\\":\\\"mthhwq\\\"}]],\\\"className\\\":\\\"lucide-chevron-right h-4 w-4\\\",\\\"aria-hidden\\\":true}]]}]]}]\\n1f:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"footer\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mt-auto border-t border-sandstone-deep/60 py-8\\\",\\\"children\\\":[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 font-ui text-xs text-ink-soft flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-2 sm:items-center sm:justify-between\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",null,{\\\"children\\\":\\\"१०८ पार्श्वनाथ तीर्थ दर्शन — श्री पार्श्वनाथ भगवान के तीर्थों का डिजिटल संकलन\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",null,{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Original source: Rajendra Kumar Jain, Parshv Sadan, Parshv Nagar, Dungarpur\\\"}]]}]}]\\n\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"24:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"11\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"63. Shri Bhabha Parshwanath\\\"}]\\n25:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"12\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Main Deity: A black-colored image of Bhagwan Shri Bhabha Parshwanath, 47 inches tall and 40 inches wide, in the padmasana posture. Above the head of the image is a canopy of 7 serpent hoods. This image is made of sand.\\\"}]\\n26:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"13\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Location: This is in Jamnagar, Saurashtra, at Chokdi, known as the Chorivala Derasar. There are 2 other temples of Bhabha Parshwanath -- one in Patan and another in Ahmedabad.\\\"}]\\n34:T448,History: Jamnagar is an ancient and important city of Saurashtra, and also a cantonment town. Shri Vardhaman Shah, Shri Padamsingh Seth, Shri Tejasvi Seth and Shri Raisingh Seth came from Bhadreshwar and settled in Jamnagar. These four Shreshthis built a beautiful and grand temple here, known as the Chorivala Temple. The attractive image of Shri Parshwanath was consecrated here (Anjanshalaka and Pratishtha) by the revered hands of Achalgacchiya Acharya Shri Kalyansagarsurishwarji, on Sunday, Vaishakh Sud Ashtami, Vikram Samvat 1678. In North Gujarat and Saurashtra, the eldest member of a household is honored with the respectful title \\\"Bha.\\\" Adding this highly respectful term to the name of Bhagwan Parshwanath as \\\"Bhabha,\\\" in order to express the greatest possible honor and respect, this image came to be addressed as \\\"Shri Bhabha Parshwanath,\\\" and hence this pilgrimage site is called Shri Bhabha Parshwanath. Most of the Jain community of Jamnagar live abroad, but they have used their wealth for all religious activities. They have built many temples in all the villages of Jamnagar.27:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"14\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"$34\\\"}]\\n28:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"15\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Other Temples: There are 16 temples here.\\\"}]\\n29:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"16\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Art and Sculpture: The temples here are very grand, artistic, striking and beautiful. The carving in the temple is highly artistic and recalls the temples of Shatrunjay Mahatirth. The images in the temple are enchanting and captivating. The colored artwork in the temple is very striking.\\\"}]\\n2a:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"17\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Directions: Jamnagar is connected by rail and road to all cities of Gujarat. Buses and private vehicles are available here. The Navanagar pilgrimage site, where the main deity is Shri Neminath Bhagwan, is 3 km from here. Dharamshala and bhojanshala facilities are available here. There is a very good and well-known Sanskrit school here, where many monks and nuns come to learn Sanskrit. The Jain community here is very devout, and many Chhari Palit Sanghs (foot pilgrimages) come here, and elaborate austerities (tapasya) are regularly organized.\\\"}]\\n2b:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"18\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Scripture References: Bhabha Parshwanath is mentioned in \\\\\\\"365 Shri Parshwa Jin Nammala,\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"Shri Shankheshwar Parshwanath Chhand,\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"Shri Bhagwan Parshwanath Stavan,\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"Shri Parshwanath Nammala,\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"Shri Parshwanath Sankhya Stavan,\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"108 Parshwanath ke Chhand,\\\\\\\" and elsewhere. There is an image of Bhabha Parshwanath at Kareda Tirth and at the Kalikund Parshwanath temple in Santacruz, Mumbai.\\\"}]\\n2c:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"19\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"1. The main temple of Bhabha Parshwanath is in Jamnagar, Saurashtra, Gujarat. 2. In Patan, there is an ancient temple of Bhabha Parshwanath at Bhabha no Pado, where Bhabha Parshwanath is the Mulanayak. The attractive image is believed to date from the time of King Sampratti. The temple's dome is filled with skillful architecture. The previous ten births of Bhagwan Parshwanath are beautifully depicted, with cloth paintings (pat) of various pilgrimage sites, done in gold tones, that catch the eye. Because of radium paint, the pat glow fluorescent at night. Elders say that at midnight the sound of bells and music used to be heard, and this divine temple was filled with the fragrance of incense and flowers. This temple was last renovated in V.S. 2015. The upashraya has a vast knowledge repository with rare and ancient books. 3. In Ahmedabad there is an ancient temple of Bhabha Parshwanath, in which the image of the Lord is small and pleasing to the eye.\\\"}]\\n2d:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"20\\\",{\\\"childr\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"en\\\":\\\"Hymn of Praise: Your well-formed body dispels the afflictions of karma; embracing you in totality, O Parshwa Bhabha, you shine forth; awakening the souls of Jamnagar, spreading friendship (maitri) -- \\\\\\\"I bow with devotion to Shri Bhabha Parshwa.\\\\\\\"\\\"}]\\n2e:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"21\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Chanting Mantra: Om Hrim Arham Shri Bhabha Parshwanathay Namah.\\\"}]\\n2f:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"22\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Trust: Shri Bhabha Parshwanath Shwetambar Jain Tirth, Seth Rishi Vardhaman Jain Pedhi, Chorivala Derasar, Post: Jamnagar, District: Saurashtra, State: Gujarat-360001, India. Phone: 0288-267840/2678923/2555946.\\\"}]\\n30:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"23\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"History of the 108 Parshwanathdada and How to Reach Them, Part 15: Having completed pilgrimages to a total of 63 shrines of Lord Parshwanath -- Navsari, Surat, Rander, Bharuch, Gandhar, Dabhoi, Chhani, Borsad, Khambhat, Kheda, Dholka, Ahmedabad, Naroda, Titoi, Modar, Baroda, Nana Posina, Mota Posina, Vijapur, Vihar, Visnagar, Mehsana, Sidhpur, Dhota Saklana, Palanpur, Bhildiyaji, Umbri, Charup, Patan (9), Mansa, Mumbai, Gambhu, Chanasma, Kambhoi, Munjpur, Shankheshwar, Shankhalpur, Mandal, Sherisa, Ghogha, Ajahara, Prabhas Patan, Chorwad, Mangrol, Bareja, Jambhanvad, and Moti Bhansal and Jamnagar -- we will now go on to visit 2 more shrines of Lord Parshwanath, one each at Bhadreshwar and Suthri. With these, the pilgrimage of 63 shrines in Gujarat and 2 in Rajasthan, totaling 65 shrines of Lord Parshwanath, is complete. From here we shall travel onward toward Rajasthan. Gandhidham to Bhadreshwar is 33 km, Bhadreshwar to Suthri is 127 km. There is regular rail service to Gandhidham from Ahmedabad.\\\"}]\\n31:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"24\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Bhadreshwar: 64. Shri Bhadreshwar Parshwanath. Suthri: 65. Shri Dhritkallol Parshwanath.\\\"}]\\n32:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"25\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Purushadani Arhat Parshwanath Charitra, Part 9 (Shri Parshwakumar's Tour of the City): One day the prince was sitting in the palace courtyard, observing the city. He saw a great crowd of people carrying worship offerings and heading out of the city. The curious Parshwakumar asked, \\\\\\\"Is there some festival today, or some other special occasion? Where are these people going?\\\\\\\" He was told, \\\\\\\"O Prince! Outside the city lives a great ascetic named Kamath, who is performing the panchagni tapas (five-fire austerity). He is a very fierce ascetic, and these people are going to worship him.\\\\\\\"\\\"}]\\n33:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"26\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"The curious prince Parshwa also went to see Kamath. This Kamath was the very same figure whose connection with Parshwanath's life had continued across many births. As soon as the prince, through his avadhignan (clairvoyant knowledge), saw a snake burning in the fire-pit along with the wood, he ordered his servant to remove the burning log from the fire and carefully split it open. When the burning log was split open, a pair of snakes -- male and female -- was found writhing, half-burnt, in their final moments of life. Parshwakumar had his servant recite the 